An **interface contract** specifies units, ranges and freshness. An **IMU** (inertial measurement unit) measures acceleration and rotation. **Specific force** is the force an accelerometer feels, such as support holding an aircraft level. **Sequence numbers** identify new samples.

The interface gives body gyro rates p/q/r in rad/s, body specific force in m/s², a sample sequence that always advances and an IMU health flag. Pilot inputs are roll/pitch radians, yaw rad/s, throttle 0–1 and an arm switch.

Outputs are four 0–1 motor commands and armed state. Fixed 250 Hz calls imply dt=4 ms, so wall-clock time is unnecessary.

The host can run roughly four simulated minutes in one real second. A repeated valid reading still becomes detectably stale through seq.

```c
typedef struct {
    float    gyro[3];
    float    accel[3];
    uint32_t seq;
    uint8_t  imu_ok;
    float    rc_roll;
    float    rc_pitch;
    float    rc_yaw_rate;
    float    rc_throttle;
    uint8_t  rc_arm;
} sensors_t;
typedef struct {
    float    motor[4];
    uint8_t  armed;
} actuators_t;
void control_step(const sensors_t *in, actuators_t *out);
```

A **complementary filter** combines fast gyro motion with slow gravity correction. **Alpha** weights the gyro prediction; **tau** is the correction’s time constant.

Both update one attitude state; τ=αdt/(1−α).

With α=0.98 and dt=0.004 s, τ=0.196 s. The complementary high-pass and low-pass contributions sum to a full estimate.

```c
float a = t->alpha;
s.roll  = a * (s.roll  + in->gyro[0] * CTRL_DT) + (1.0f - a) * s.roll_acc;
s.pitch = a * (s.pitch + in->gyro[1] * CTRL_DT) + (1.0f - a) * s.pitch_acc;
```

**Windup** is integral error accumulating at an actuator limit. **Anti-windup** stops or bounds it. A **saturated output** has reached its limit.

Conditional integration stops when either axis output or mixed motors saturate. When allowed, the accumulator is bounded by i_limit.

Disarm or zero throttle resets every integrator. In the host restraint trial (2 s, 30° request), the table shows anti-windup prevents stored correction overturning the released robot.

| anti-windup | peak roll | within 2° of 30° by | end state |
|---|---|---|---|
| on | 29.8° | 0.28 s | flying |
| **off** | **179.5°** | never | **flipped; tilt failsafe** |

```c
if (!t->anti_windup) {
    *integ += t->rate_ki * e * CTRL_DT;
} else if (u == u_free && !motors_saturated) {
    *integ = f_clamp(*integ + t->rate_ki * e * CTRL_DT, -t->i_limit, t->i_limit);
}
```

**Collective throttle** raises all motors; **differential commands** raise some and lower others. **Negative feedback** opposes error; **positive feedback** increases it. **FL/FR/RR/RL** mean front-left/front-right/rear-right/rear-left.

The X mixer is the inverse thrust-to-torque map. Its roll column is +,−,−,+.

Pitch is +,+,−,−. Yaw is −,+,−,+ for FL/FR/RR/RL.

Adding collective throttle preserves average thrust. Reversing mix_roll_sign converts negative feedback to positive feedback.

Host hover trips tilt after 0.36 s with the reversed sign and then crashes. Real sign checks should be done with propellers removed.

```c
m[0] = th + r + u_p - u_y;
m[1] = th - r + u_p + u_y;
m[2] = th - r - u_p - u_y;
m[3] = th + r - u_p + u_y;
```

The shell change `mix 1` to `mix -1` reverses the roll mixer sign.

**XOR** toggles pixels; applying the same mask twice restores them. **SIM** is simulated-sensor mode, which exposes true attitude.

For each x pixel, render ground below y=32+pitch_in_degrees−(x−32)tan(roll). Right roll makes the horizon rise at its right end.

Positive pitch moves it down one pixel per degree. XOR keeps the aircraft symbol visible.

SIM overlays dotted true attitude, exposing estimator error. Real flight lacks that truth.

The other half shows state, motor layout FL FR/RL RR, angles, gain, α and observed loop/frame rates.

```c
float s = f_sin(roll), c = f_cos(roll);
float slope = s / c, cy = 32.0f + pitch * DEG;
for (int x = 1; x < 63; x++) {
    int y = (int)(cy - ((float)x - 32.0f) * slope);
    oled_vline(x, y, 63 - y, OLED_ON);
}
```

A **residual** is measurement minus prediction. **Alpha/beta** are correction gains: larger values follow GPS jitter; smaller values permit acceleration drift.

At each 20 ms step, integrate acceleration to velocity and then position. Every new 100 ms GPS fix applies a residual correction: position α=0.25 and velocity β=0.04/0.1 s=0.4 s⁻¹.

EST_A/EST_B tune the fixed-gain estimator. Pure 0.03 m/s² bias gives 0.5 a t²=1.5 m drift after 10 s.

```c
fc.vx += s->acc_x * dt;
fc.px += fc.vx * dt;
fc.px += a * rx;
fc.vx += b * rx;
```

A **barometer** measures air pressure. A **complementary filter** combines fast prediction with a slower reference. **Damping** controls oscillation decay.

Altitude uses barometer residual r=baro−pz, vz+=(acc_z+4 r)dt and pz+=(vz+2.8 r)dt. Thus natural frequency is 2 rad/s and damping ratio 0.7, filtering barometer noise above roughly 0.3 Hz while correcting long-term acceleration drift.

There is no barometer delay in this model. Host normal touchdowns are 0.41–0.43 m/s.

Descent logic uses estimated height and motion to slow and recognise landing.

```c
float r = s->baro_alt - fc.pz;
fc.vz += (s->acc_z + 4.0f * r) * dt;
fc.pz += (fc.vz    + 2.8f * r) * dt;
```

Yaw changes which body direction corresponds to east. **atan2** finds an angle from two components. **Out-of-line functions** share one implementation across source files.

Yaw rotates desired world acceleration into forward/right axes. Right uses ax sin(yaw)−ay cos(yaw) under this convention.

Add gravity to vertical acceleration, take vector magnitude as thrust, atan 2(forward, up) as pitch and atan 2(right,√(forward²+up²)) as roll. This inverts the tilted-thrust plant.

The m_ math functions are shared out-of-line fmath wrappers, compiled once in fm.c.

```c
float cy = m_cos(fc.yaw), sy = m_sin(fc.yaw);
float a_fwd   = ax * cy + ay * sy;
float a_right = ax * sy - ay * cy;
float a_up    = GRAVITY + az;
o->thrust   = m_sqrt(a_fwd * a_fwd + a_right * a_right + a_up * a_up);
o->pitch    = m_atan2(a_fwd, a_up);
o->roll     = m_atan2(a_right, m_sqrt(a_fwd * a_fwd + a_up * a_up));
```

**Along-track** means along the route; **cross-track** means sideways from it. Separate bounds preserve sideways steering while cruising.

Project waypoint error onto along-track and cross-track unit vectors, then bound each component separately by cruise speed. A controller aiming directly at the waypoint bounds the whole velocity vector, leaving little sideways correction far from a waypoint.

Host 8 m/s wind results: line guidance 2.07 m max/1.00 m RMS cross-track versus point aim 2.34/1.13 m, at 0.2 s extra duration.

```c
float along  = ex * tx + ey * ty;
float across = ex * -ty + ey * tx;
float va = clampf(fc.p.kp_pos * along,  fc.p.cruise);
float vc = clampf(fc.p.kp_pos * across, fc.p.cruise);
```

The parameter LINE=0 selects point aiming. LINE=1 selects separated along-track/cross-track guidance.

A **fence** bounds the permitted region. Critical battery demands local landing before mission or return commands.

Run failsafes every 20 ms before ordinary mode logic. Critical battery takes precedence and immediately LANDs with a return from the safety function.

Low battery or exceeding radial/altitude fence requests RTL from TAKEOFF/HOLD/MISSION. Fence checks use estimated coordinates.

Host scenarios show 50 m fence overshoot to 51.6 m and home landing 0.45 m away; 32% start triggers RTL at 20.1 s and lands with 17.1%. A 9% drop triggers local landing with 4.5% remaining.

| Test | Result |
|---|---|
| joystick pushes east at 3 m/s past the 50 m fence | RTL (`FENCE`) at 29.9 s; farthest **51.6 m**; landed **0.45 m** from home |
| start at 32 %, fly the mission | RTL (`BAT`) at 20.1 s, mission abandoned; landed at home (0.19 m) with **17.1 %** |
| battery drops to 9 % at 25 s, mid-mission | **LAND in place** (`BATCRIT`), no RTL; touched down 0.6 m from there at 0.39 m/s with **4.5 %** left |

```c
if (fc.battery < fc.p.bat_crit && m != M_LAND) {
    enter(M_LAND, R_BATTERY_CRIT);
    return;
}
if (m == M_TAKEOFF || m == M_HOLD || m == M_MISSION) {
    if (fc.battery < fc.p.bat_low) { enter(M_RTL, R_BATTERY); return; }
    float r2 = fc.px * fc.px + fc.py * fc.py;
    if (r2 > fc.p.fence_r * fc.p.fence_r || fc.pz > fc.p.fence_alt) {
        enter(M_RTL, R_FENCE);
    }
}
```

**SysTick** counts CPU cycles downward within a kernel tick. Retry if the tick changes while reading its counter.

SysTick LOAD=47999 and VAL bits 23:0 count down at 48 MHz. Read (os_ticks, VAL), retry if ticks changed, and calculate elapsed cycles as Δms×(LOAD+1)+oldVAL−newVAL. stats reports last/worst/mean control and world µs and CPU shares.

Target timings were not measured: disassembly counts 114 soft-float calls in control, roughly 50–100 cycles each, predicting hundreds of microseconds within 20 ms.

| Register / function | Width, value and bit fields |
|---|---|
| SysTick->LOAD  —  47 999: one tick per ms at 48 MHz | 32 = 0x0000BB7F; 23:0 RELOAD |
| SysTick->VAL  —  counts DOWN from LOAD to 0 | 32; 23:0 CURRENT |

```c
static stamp_t stamp(void)
{
    stamp_t s;
    do { s.ms = os_ticks();  s.val = SysTick->VAL; } while (s.ms != os_ticks());
    return s;
}
static uint32_t cycles(stamp_t a, stamp_t b)
{
    return (b.ms - a.ms) * (SysTick->LOAD + 1u) + a.val - b.val;
}
```

**Reflectance** measures light reflected by the floor. Dark tape and light floor yield different readings across the seven-sensor bar.

Seven reflectance values span 0–1000, with black near 1000 and white near 100. Sensor 0 is rightmost and 6 leftmost.

Position is (i−3)×13 mm to the left. Inputs also include cumulative wheel counts, knob 0–1000 and sample time ms.

Two integer output speeds are ±2000 mm/s. Control runs at 100 Hz and may use float internally while keeping the sensor and motor units as integers at the interface.

```c
typedef struct {
    uint16_t line[LINE_SENSORS];
    int32_t  enc_left, enc_right;
    int16_t  knob;
    uint32_t t_ms;
} sensors_t;
typedef struct {
    int16_t left, right;
} actuators_t;
void control_step(const sensors_t *in, actuators_t *out);
```

**Tau** is a first-order response’s time constant. A **slew limit** bounds speed change per second.

Each wheel follows its requested speed with τ=40 ms but wheel acceleration is limited to ±8000 mm/s². Update speed by a clamped first-order increment.

A pure first-order step reaches 63% after one τ, subject here to the slew cap. Host standing-start first laps are about 80 ms slower than following laps.

An ideal instantaneous motor would conceal this and encourage excessive gain.

```c
    float dl = f_clamp((ul - world.wl) * (dt / MOTOR_TAU_S), -MOTOR_ACC_MMS2 * dt, MOTOR_ACC_MMS2 * dt);
    float dr = f_clamp((ur - world.wr) * (dt / MOTOR_TAU_S), -MOTOR_ACC_MMS2 * dt, MOTOR_ACC_MMS2 * dt);
    world.wl += dl;
    world.wr += dr;
```

**Lateral** means sideways. The **friction circle** limits combined forward/sideways acceleration, so braking in a turn consumes grip.

Tyre acceleration magnitude is limited to μg=0.7 g=6867 mm/s². The body heading follows wheel differential, but ground velocity is pulled toward that heading only as fast as grip allows.

Above the friction circle, normalise ax/ay back to the circle and mark slipping. Corner speed obeys v≤√(0.7 gR).

```c
    float ax = TYRE_K * (v * c - world.vx), ay = TYRE_K * (v * s - world.vy);
    float a2 = ax * ax + ay * ay;
    world.slipping = (uint8_t)(a2 > MU_G_MMS2 * MU_G_MMS2);
    if (world.slipping) {
        float k = MU_G_MMS2 / f_sqrt(a2);
```

A **centroid** is a weighted average of sensor positions. Subtract the white-floor threshold before estimating tape position.

Compute positive weights max(reading−threshold,0), then line position as weighted sensor-position mean. At threshold 300, sensor 4 contributes 350×13 and sensor 3 contributes 600×0.

Total 950 gives 4.8 mm left. Subtracting white prevents background votes.

Interpolation gives millimetre precision from 13 mm sensor pitch.

| Sensor | y (mm) | distance | reading | weight (− 300) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 4 | +13 | 8 mm | 650 | 350 |
| 3 | 0 | 5 mm | 900 | 600 |
| 2 | −13 | 18 mm | 100 | — |

```c
        float w = (float)in->line[i] - g->thr;
        if (w <= 0.0f) { continue; }
        float y = (float)((i - (LINE_SENSORS - 1) / 2) * SENSOR_PITCH_MM);
        sw  += w;
        swy += w * y;
```

**PID** means proportional, integral and derivative control. D damps changes but reacts to noise. **Lookahead** is sensor-bar distance ahead of the wheels.

PID turns with kp=20, kd=1.0, ki=0 and derivative low-pass factor 0.5 by default. Derive error difference per dt, low-pass it, then left=v−turn/right=v+turn.

Sensor bar lookahead already supplies 70/v seconds of heading lead: 70 ms at 1 m/s but 50 ms at 1.4 m/s, against 40 ms motors. Faster motion erodes natural lead, making D necessary to prevent weaving.

```c
    float d_raw = have_prev ? (e - e_prev) / dt : 0.0f;
    d_filt += g->dfilt * (d_raw - d_filt);
    ...
    float turn = g->kp * e + g->ki * i_acc + g->kd * d_filt;
```

A centred loss may be a tape gap; an edge loss suggests a missed corner. Many dark sensors can indicate a crossing.

Base speed decreases by slow×clamp(|error|/half-bar,0,1), with default slow=0.5. If tape vanished near centre, coast straight up to 400 ms to cross a gap.

If last seen at an edge, spin toward that side to recover a missed corner. Five or more dark sensors indicate crossing: retain previous error and suppress D/I updates.

```c
    float v = base * (1.0f - g->slow * f_clamp(f_abs(e) / HALF_BAR_MM, 0.0f, 1.0f));
```

```c
        if (f_abs(last_pos) < GAP_POS_MM && lost_ms <= COAST_MS) {
            ctl_state.lost = 1;
```

An **FPU** is a floating-point unit; Cortex-M0+ uses software arithmetic. **SysTick** is the kernel’s repeating hardware counter.

A PC with an FPU cannot establish M0+ timing. SysTick LOAD=47999 bits 23:0, VAL bits 23:0 counts downward.

Cycles_now combines os_ticks×48000 with elapsed within-tick cycles, retrying a crossed tick. World must finish within 240000 cycles or 5 ms.

A few-percent cost is only an expectation. Target stats is the required measurement.

```c
static uint32_t cycles_now(void)
{
    uint32_t ms, val;
    do { ms = os_ticks(); val = SysTick->VAL; } while (ms != os_ticks());
    return ms * (SysTick->LOAD + 1u) + (SysTick->LOAD - val);
}
```

| Register / function | Width, value and bit fields |
|---|---|
| LOAD  —  SYST_RVR | 32 = 0x0000BB7F; 23:0 RELOAD |
| VAL  —  SYST_CVR, counts DOWN | 32; 23:0 CURRENT |

The **interface contract** supplies measured encoders, gyro, ranges and bumper. **CCW** means counterclockwise; **milliradians** are thousandths of a radian.

The 20 ms world publishes a 28-byte sensor structure: time, cumulative encoders, integer CCW gyro milliradians/s with bias, five mm ranges and bumper. The 40 ms nav task returns 4-byte wheel commands. nav.c never includes world.h, keeping real true map and pose unavailable.

World models motor lag, wheel mismatches, beam noise, collisions and a changing door.

```c
typedef struct {
    uint32_t t_ms;
    int32_t  enc_l, enc_r;
    int32_t  gyro_mrad_s;
    uint16_t range_mm[N_RANGE];
    uint8_t  bumper;
} sensors_t;
```

**Dead reckoning** adds measured motion to the previous pose. Accumulated gyro bias can displace mapped walls.

Wheel count differences become dl/dr millimetres, mean ds gives travel, and calibrated gyro integration gives dθ. Midpoint heading eth+0.5 dθ projects ds to x/y.

Wheel-derived heading is an optional contrast. A 1.5% relative wheel error over 1 m creates about 0.25 rad heading error using 60 mm wheelbase.

In 25 host runs, calibrated gyro wins (table). Heading error compounds later position/map errors.

| heading from | arrived | estimate error at the end (mean / worst) |
|---|---|---|
| gyro, calibrated (default) | **25 / 25** | 9 mm / 20 mm |
| gyro, **not** calibrated | 16 / 25 | 144 mm / 476 mm |
| **wheels** | **0 / 25** | 692 mm / 1172 mm |

```c
float dl = (float)(in->enc_l - prev_l) * (1000.0f / TICKS_PER_M);
float dr = (float)(in->enc_r - prev_r) * (1000.0f / TICKS_PER_M);
float ds  = 0.5f * (dl + dr);
float dth = (P.heading == HEAD_WHEELS)
          ? (dr - dl) / (float)WHEELBASE_MM
          : ((float)in->gyro_mrad_s * 0.001f - gyro_bias) * dt;
float mid = eth + 0.5f * dth;
ex += ds * m_cos(mid);
ey += ds * m_sin(mid);
```

Average gyro offset while stationary. Motion during calibration would be subtracted later as false bias; averaging suppresses independent noise.

Stand still for 50 samples at 25 Hz (2 s), average gyro readings and subtract the estimated bias thereafter. Independent 2 mrad/s noise averages to roughly 2/√50≈0.28 mrad/s.

An earlier 25-sample/3 mrad/s design produced one poor seed in 10, about 2.4 instead of 4 mrad/s, yielding 6° drift/min and a misplaced wall. Renode level 1 reported 4020 µrad/s.

```c
if (mode == NAV_CALIB) {
    cal_sum += in->gyro_mrad_s;
    if (++cal_n >= CAL_SAMPLES) {
        gyro_bias = (float)cal_sum / (float)cal_n * 0.001f;
```

A **heap** retrieves the smallest search score efficiently. **Decrease-key** updates a candidate when a cheaper route is found, avoiding duplicates.

A* uses g uint16[448]=896 B, packed closed/parent-direction info 448 B, byte heap positions 448 B and 160 uint16 heap entries 320 B:2112 B. Parent directions need 3 bits.

Decrease-key moves an existing entry rather than duplicates. Packed (f, h, cell) unsigned key provides deterministic ties.

Total stated nav state 3288 B includes map 448, costmap 448, path 256, A*2112, vetoes 24. Lazy duplicate insertion overflowed 255 slots after 173/335 cells and falsely returned no path, so memory saving must preserve correctness.

```c
static uint16_t g[GRID_N];
static uint8_t  info[GRID_N];
static uint8_t  hpos[GRID_N];
static uint16_t heap[ASTAR_HEAP];
```

A three-bit direction replaces each 16-bit parent-cell index.

New obstacles can invalidate a route or its diagonal corner cells. Recheck both endpoints and adjacent corner cells.

Plan when requested or path is empty. Invalidate remaining route if a new wall occupies it, a new wall forbids a diagonal corner, or distance from upcoming route cells exceeds 220 mm.

This recomputes A*, without incremental D* state. Backoffs, brakes or estimate shifts can invalidate tracking.

Host trap initially enters its U then discovers escape:2.8 replans/run and 0 collisions.

```c
if (need_plan || path_len == 0u) {
    plan(S.plans > 0u);
} else if (plan_invalid() || ...) {
    plan(1);
}
```

A **veto** temporarily rejects a path cell after persistent braking. Expiry permits reconsidering transient blockages.

After 1.5 s blocked, veto the next path cell for 20 s and replan, allowing reactive safety to inform deliberative routing. If no path remains, first clear vetoes, then discard weak walls seen only once/twice, before declaring NOPATH.

This recovery avoids trusting one noisy map too strongly. A sealed goal still reports NOPATH after 23.9 s host exploration.

```c
if (in->t_ms - blocked_since > STUCK_MS) {
    veto_cell[veto_next]  = path[path_i + 1u];
    veto_until[veto_next] = in->t_ms + VETO_MS;
    need_plan = 1;
}
```

The original deadlock appeared in level 5 when the plan selected a one-cell gap that the reactive layer refused.

A **uint32 counter** wraps at its maximum. Unsigned subtraction preserves short elapsed intervals across wrap.

nav_clock retries if a millisecond tick changes while reading VAL. Kernel SysTick LOAD=47999=0xBB7F, RELOAD/CURRENT bits 23:0.

Elapsed-in-tick is LOAD−VAL. Combine ticks×48000 for a 48 MHz software cycle counter because M0+ lacks DWT CYCCNT.

The uint32 counter wraps about 89 s, but unsigned subtraction preserves short elapsed intervals across wrap.

```c
uint32_t nav_clock(void)
{
    uint32_t ms, val;
    do {
        ms  = os_ticks();
        val = SysTick->VAL;
    } while (ms != os_ticks());
    return ms * (SysTick->LOAD + 1u) + (SysTick->LOAD - val);
}
```

| Register / function | Width, value and bit fields |
|---|---|
| SysTick->LOAD  —  set by the kernel: 48 MHz / 1000 - 1 | 32 = 0x0000BB7F; 23:0 RELOAD |
| SysTick->VAL  —  counts DOWN to 0, once per ms | 32; 23:0 CURRENT |

Cortex-M0+ lacks `DWT->CYCCNT`.

An **IMU** is an inertial measurement unit; an **encoder** counts wheel rotation. **PWM** (pulse-width modulation) controls motor drive through a reversible-voltage **H-bridge**.

Inputs are biased/noisy gyro pitch rate, forward/up specific force, encoder counts relative to the body and joystick speed m/s. Output is the common motor voltage, physically clipped to±VBAT. control.c includes only control/params/math headers, never world.h.

A real IMU, encoder-mode timer and PWM H-bridge could replace world.c without altering control. The host links the identical controller.

```c
typedef struct {
    float   gyro;
    float   acc_f;
    float   acc_u;
    int32_t enc;
    float   v_cmd;
} sensors_t;
typedef struct {
    float   volts;
} actuators_t;
void control_step(const sensors_t *in, actuators_t *out);
```

**Semi-implicit Euler** updates velocity before position. An **FPU** is a floating-point unit; software arithmetic and smaller steps cost CPU.

Solve the 2×2 mass/coupling system, update velocities, then positions using the new velocities: semi-implicit/symplectic Euler. Ordinary old-velocity Euler adds energy to an undriven pendulum.

At 1 ms, a roughly 14 ms fastest time constant still has 14 steps. RK 2 doubles equation evaluation and soft-float cost for unnecessary accuracy here.

Fmath avoids about 4 KB heavyweight trig support.

```c
w->xdd  = (a22 * b1 - a12 * b2) / det;
w->thdd = (a11 * b2 - a12 * b1) / det;
w->xd  += w->xdd  * WORLD_DT;
w->thd += w->thdd * WORLD_DT;
w->x   += w->xd   * WORLD_DT;
w->th  += w->thd  * WORLD_DT;
```

**CPR** means counts per revolution. Add body tilt to encoder angle for ground-relative wheel position; filter noisy differentiated speed.

Ground wheel angle equals encoder angle plus estimated body tilt. Multiply by 35 mm radius to estimate position.

Differentiate at 5 ms to estimate speed then low-pass by factor 0.25, about 17 ms lag. One 1440-CPR count gives roughly 0.15 mm, so one count/5 ms is 0.03 m/s and raw speed is quantised.

```c
float wheel = (float)in->enc * (F_2PI / ENC_CPR) + est.th;
est.x = BOT_R * wheel;
float raw = (est.x - x_prev) * (1.0f / CTRL_DT);
est.xd += XD_FILTER * (raw - est.xd);
```

**PID** means proportional/integral/derivative control; a **cascade** nests loops. **LQR** means linear quadratic regulator, a full-state feedback design. **Slew limits** slow command changes.

PID tilt usesθ, integral andrate. Cascade maps position/speed errors to lean then lean/rate to volts.

LQR uses all four states together. All share estimation and joystick command slew 0.5 m/s².

Before any law, estimated tilt outside±50° resets I and outputs 0 V, preventing powered wheels on a fallen robot. This estimate-based trip still cannot detect an error hidden in its estimate.

| | sees | output | gains from |
|---|---|---|---|
| **PID tilt** | θ, ∫θ, θ̇ | `u = KP θ + KI ∫θ + KD θ̇` | hand-tuned |
| **cascade** | x, ẋ → a lean; θ, θ̇ → volts | two PDs, one inside the other | hand-tuned |
| **LQR** | [x, ẋ, θ, θ̇] all at once | `u = −K x` | **computed** by `lqr.py` |

```c
if (est.th > TRIP_ANGLE || est.th < -TRIP_ANGLE) {
    integ = 0.0f;
    out->volts = 0.0f;
    return;
}
```

The outer loop requests lean from position/speed; the inner loop requests voltage. A moving reference can accumulate error against a lean clamp.

Outer PD chooses lean from position/speed errors and clamps it. Inner PD uses tilt error with 25 andgyro rate gain 2.

Tune fast inner first. A±0.10 rad lean clamp failed at 0.3 m/s: moving reference kept accumulating error, the robot overshot to 1 m/s and ran out of voltage.

Allowing±0.30 rad succeeded. A setpoint clamp is a nonlinearity that can create windup-like error accumulation even without an I term.

```c
float lean = CAS_KPX * (est.x_ref - est.x) + CAS_KVX * (est.v_ref - est.xd);
est.th_ref = f_clamp(lean, -CAS_LEAN, CAS_LEAN);
u = CAS_KP * (est.th - est.th_ref) + CAS_KD * in->gyro;
```

The failed ±0.10 rad clamp is about ±6°.

**DIST_NONE** marks an unusable range reading. Signed motor percentages command forward/reverse. Strategy state belongs only in the supplied private buffer.

