An **impulse** is force accumulated over a collision. **Restitution** controls bounce; **tangential friction** acts along contact and can spin the disc.

Disc collision uses normal impulse with restitution 0.1 and a tangential impulse bounded by body friction coefficient multiplied by normal impulse. Tangential force at the radius spins a robot hit at an angle.

Overlap is split between both bodies. Continued pushing emerges from repeated motor acceleration, impulses and tyre resistance.

The simplified disc omits real wedges, scoops, corners and lifting an opponent's tyres.

