A **latched flag** retains an impact until consumed. Contact directions are relative to the robot’s front/back/left/right.

Rotate the contact normal into body coordinates. A forward component above 0.7071 selects FRONT, below −0.7071 BACK, otherwise its lateral sign selects LEFT or RIGHT.

Contact flags latch any hit since the last control read and clear when consumed. Reorient the nose toward dangerous side hits.

```c
static uint8_t bump_side(const body_t *b, float nx, float ny)
{
    float lx =  nx * c + ny * s;
    float ly = -nx * s + ny * c;
    if (lx >  0.7071f) { return BUMP_FRONT; }
    if (lx < -0.7071f) { return BUMP_BACK; }
    return ly > 0.0f ? BUMP_LEFT : BUMP_RIGHT;
}
```

The 0.7071 forward-normal threshold defines the front/back sectors within 45° of their axes.

Both brains must sense the same world instant before either robot moves. Alternating sides further reduces ordering bias.

First sense both robots, then run both brains, then apply both commands. Interleaving robot zero's movement before robot one's sensing would give the latter newer information.

Tournament sides alternate: the student is robot zero on even matches and robot one on odd matches. This reduces sequential-simulation bias.

