**SysTick** counts cycles downward. A **pending interrupt** has been requested but not serviced; a wrapped tick may not yet reach kernel bookkeeping.

SysTick LOAD is 47999 and VAL counts down. Meaningful counter fields are bits 23:0.

Read tick, VAL and ICSR PENDSTSET bit 26 with interrupts masked. A pending tick with VAL above half-load indicates wrap before tick bookkeeping, so increment the sampled tick.

Timestamp is tick×48000+(47999−VAL). Other ICSR fields are listed in the register table below.

| Register / function | Width, value and bit fields |
|---|---|
| LOAD  —  SYST_RVR | 32 = 0x0000BB7F; 23:0 RELOAD |
| VAL  —  SYST_CVR, counts DOWN | 32; 23:0 CURRENT |
| ICSR  —  SCB | 32; 31 NMIPENDSET, 28 PENDSVSET, 27 PENDSVCLR, 26 PENDSTSET, 25 PENDSTCLR, 23 ISRPREEMPT, 22 ISRPENDING, 17:12 VECTPENDING, 5:0 VECTACTIVE |

```c
__disable_irq();
uint32_t t = os_ticks();
uint32_t v = SysTick->VAL;
uint32_t pend = SCB->ICSR & SCB_ICSR_PENDSTSET_Msk;
__enable_irq();
if (pend && v > load / 2u) { t++; }
return t * (load + 1u) + (load - v);
```

Cortex-M3 and later provide `DWT->CYCCNT`. Cortex-M0+ does not; `core_cm0plus.h` defines no DWT block, so this design measures through SysTick instead.

A **cycle** is one CPU clock interval. **Soft-float** uses software arithmetic. Average execution cost cannot bound the slowest strategy call.

The strategy peak budget is 20000 cycles, about 417 µs at 48 MHz. The referee measures every call and includes its worst in RESULT.

Over-budget entries are rejected. Roughly 50–100 cycles per soft-float operation permits hundreds of arithmetic operations or a state machine, not unbounded search.

Include rare edge reactions in peak measurements.

